    thinking: "**Analyzing Error Messages**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting the specific error messages from the diagnostic output. It looks like \"gemini-3.8-flash\" is hitting a 503 error \342\200\223 indicating high demand. \"gemini-4-argon,\" on the other hand, is returning a 429 error. I\'m trying to determine the specific source of these errors.\n\n\n**Confirming Problem & Solution**\n\nI\'ve got a breakthrough: the errors are directly tied to quota limits and high demand. A 503 error, as expected, translates to \"high demand,\" and the server returns clean JSON detailing the issue. Before, these errors triggered a messy HTML response, but now the update addresses this. My frontend code now correctly handles both 503 and 429 errors safely, showing that the server is busy.\n\n\n**Implementing Retry Strategy**\n\nI\'ve implemented a robust retry mechanism with backoff, ensuring the app never crashes with invalid JSON. The \"Coba Lagi\" button handles 503 and 429 errors gracefully, and it seamlessly recovers when the upstream issue resolves. Also, the strict adherence to not providing fake results is crucial for us here.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Fallback Models**\n\nI\'m now focusing on model fallback strategies. Given regional load spikes, I\'ve prioritized specific fallback models: \"gemini-3.1-flash-lite\" and then \"gemini-flash-latest,\" to maximize the chances of connecting with a non-busy endpoint. This should significantly improve resilience. The checklist items have all been addressed, including the UI and AI-related points.\n\n\n"
