  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing USC Data**\n\nI have a complete picture of the data from `RAB Gedung USC.xlsx` thanks to the JSON and TypeScript files. My next step is a careful comparison between all these files. I need to make sure the data in them are aligned. I will need to inspect the data for discrepancies.\n\n\n**Reviewing USC Data Details**\n\nI am now meticulously examining the USC workbook\'s sections, specifically focusing on \"Kelompok Umum & Pondasi\" and its subsections like \"Pekerjaan Persiapan\" and \"Pekerjaan Tanah dan Urugan.\" I am cross-referencing this with the `uscCatalog.ts` file, ensuring each element aligns perfectly with the specifications defined in the prompt. I\'m focusing on discrepancies and confirming requirements are met.\n\n\n**Detailing Foundation Work**\n\nI am now focusing on the structural foundation elements within the USC data. Specifically, I am analyzing \"Pengurugan Tanah Peninggian,\" \"Pengurugan Pasir,\" and various stone and concrete foundation types. I\'m cross-referencing these descriptions with their corresponding rows and identifying the units used. I am examining volumes and material specifications, verifying all details align with the overall project requirements.\n\n\n**Examining Foundation Structure**\n\nI am now closely examining the structural elements associated with footplat foundations, specifically focusing on Type B. I\'ve noted the concrete volume, reinforcing steel weight, and formwork area, linking these details to the appropriate rows. I\'m also scrutinizing the pedestal columns, including concrete volume, reinforcement (main and stirrups), and formwork.\n\n\n**Detailing Pedestal Columns**\n\nI am now focusing on the pedestal columns, specifically Type K2 with dimensions of 40x40 cm, and analyzing the corresponding rows (59-63). I have analyzed the concrete volume, reinforcement, and formwork area. I\'m also examining the sloof elements, including S1, S2 and S3, and taking note of the concrete and reinforcement details. I am analyzing the relevant units of measure. Finally, I\'ve started examining the MEP and Landscape sections, focusing on the Bio Septic Tank installation.\n\n\n**Examining Floor Structure**\n\nI am now focusing on the structural elements associated with the first, second and other floors. I am examining the structural columns, specifically analyzing `str-kolom-k1-beton`, `str-kolom-k1-tulangan`, and `str-kolom-k1-begel`, and noting the unit of measurement used for each element, and focusing on volume and weight. I\'m taking note of the concrete and reinforcing details of each element, making sure to identify associated rows.\n\n\n**Detailing Column Structures**\n\nI am now focusing on column details, specifically analyzing Type K2 and K3 columns, alongside practical columns. I\'ve noted the reinforcing steel and formwork, including their associated units, for K2 columns. I am also moving into the analysis of beam details including B1 and B2, including their concrete volume, reinforcing steel, and formwork area, and the units of measurement used for each element. This includes all rows from 78-92.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing Beam Structures**\n\nI am now closely analyzing the remaining beam details. I\'m focusing on the reinforcing steel for stirrups/ties (`kg`) in rows 93-94, formwork for B2 beams (`m\302\262`) in row 95. I\'ve also identified beam types B2 Kantilever/B3 (`m\302\263`), and the Lintel beam and its corresponding reinforcement details (`kg`), formwork (`m\302\262`), and Bordes beams with concrete volumes, and reinforcement details (`kg`) and formwork (`m\302\262`) on the stairs. I\'m verifying the units of measurement for each element. Finally, I\'m noting the Ring Beams\' concrete volume (`m\302\263`).\n\n\n**Detailing Staircase Structure**\n\nI am now focusing on the staircase structure, specifically the concrete treads and landings, noting the volume in cubic meters. I\'ve moved on to the reinforcing steel for the treads and landings, noting the quantity in kilograms. I am examining the formwork details for both, in square meters. I am now looking at the concrete for the landings and stairs, measuring it in cubic meters, then examining the floor slabs and their concrete and reinforcement details. I am also moving into the atap structure.\n\n\n\n**Examining Architectural Details**\n\nI\'m now analyzing architectural details, starting with the installation of Lisplank GRC, specifically examining the details in Row 337. I\'m focusing on the architectural volume across various floors. I\'ve begun to analyze the wall installation and plastering work, including Hebel walls, plastering with various specifications, acian, and ceramic tiles for toilets. I am taking note of the measurements and rows of each element.\n\n\n**Reviewing Architectural Finishes**\n\nI am now focusing on finishing details. I\'m noting the Homogenous Tile installation, the Anti-Slip Ceramic tiles in toilets, and the Wood-Motif Ceramic tiles for stairs, measuring them in square meters. I\'m also observing the concrete floor with acian finishing. Finally, I\'ve started reviewing the painting and gypsum ceiling installations, making sure the measurements and units align.\n\n\n**Examining Door & Window**\n\nI\'m now diving into the details of doors and windows. I\'m focusing on the installation of Type P1 through P4 doors, and J1 through J4 windows, meticulously noting the units for each and the corresponding rows. I\'m verifying that all data, including materials and accessories, are accounted for in these elements.\n\n\n**Detailing Doors, Windows & MEP**\n\nI am currently focusing on detailing windows, doors and MEP elements within the USC data. I am noting details for J4 windows, Bouvenlights, and railings. I\'ve also started to analyze the MEP installations per floor, particularly the points of light, wall outlets, light switches, toilets, washbasins, and faucets. I am carefully documenting each element\'s unit and its corresponding row.\n\n\n**Mapping MEP Data**\n\nI am now focusing on the mapping of the MEP data. I\'m noting the details of the water pipes, specifically PVC clean water pipes (\303\230 1/2\" - 1\") and sewage pipes (\303\230 3\" - 4\"), noting their respective rows and measurements. I am now exploring the mapping in `/src/services/projectStorage.ts` for each floor and cross-referencing this with the `uscCatalog.ts` file, particularly the general and foundation floor rules. I\'m investigating if the \'-kustom\' items in the `uscCatalog.ts` are a part of the USC workbook or are generic custom items.\n\n\n**Verifying Custom Structure Data**\n\nI am now focusing on the custom structural elements. I\'m carefully comparing the \"Pondasi Footplat Kustom,\" \"Sloof Kustom,\" \"Kolom Struktur Kustom,\" and \"Balok Struktur Kustom\" elements with the core USC template. I am cross-referencing these custom components with the full USC catalog to ensure they are fully aligned with the requirements. I am carefully analyzing the data for discrepancies.\n\n\n\n**Revising Structure Alignment**\n\nI am now focused on integrating all USC work structure details. I am re-reading the requirements and cross-referencing this against `projectStorage.ts` for catalog IDs. I\'m verifying the inclusion of all general and floor work groups, including preparation, structural, architectural, and MEP elements. My current task is to ensure the catalog IDs are mapped to the correct job structure to avoid replacing catalog items with a simpler structure.\n\n\n**Fixing Work Initialization**\n\nI have identified critical flaws in how projects initialize work items, particularly in `projectStorage.ts`. The system can either incorrectly load incomplete work items or overwrite user-defined ones, especially in previously saved projects. I am currently revising the logic to ensure a full catalog is loaded and to preserve any existing user customizations, improving data integrity.\n\n\n**Correcting Template Integration**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a crucial flaw: the system inadvertently deletes the USC template, preserving only user-created or simplified work. The root cause is a faulty logic in `projectStorage.ts`, where the project initialization incorrectly loads and overwrites data. I am now revising the initialization process to ensure the USC template always takes precedence, correctly integrating the full catalog.\n\n\n**Tracing Data Load Process**\n\nI have a much clearer understanding of the data loading sequence. It\'s now apparent that the logic in `projectStorage.ts` incorrectly filters out the `Gedung USC` project during initialization, leading to its deletion. Furthermore, existing projects fail to populate the complete USC template due to checks on `projectWorks.length`, compounded by the use of simplified default catalogs for new projects. I\'m verifying the fix against the root causes.\n\n\n**Fixing Template Loading**\n\nI am now focusing on fully integrating the provided work catalog within `/src/data/uscCatalog.ts`. I am ensuring every section of the template is preserved and accurately populated across the various floor levels. I am making sure the load functions handle all items for proper initialization and template recovery. My primary aim is to ensure the complete USC reference is properly loaded, including the initial template, to fix the deletion issue.\n\n\n**Integrating Floor Level Data**\n\nI am now focusing on integrating floor-specific architectural, MEP, and structural data, specifically for the second and other floors. I am reviewing the logic in `projectStorage.ts` to include the `per_lantai` USC work items across all floors. My current task is to ensure the architectural data, especially for the roof level, aligns with the specified floor-level structures.\n\n\n**Improving Catalog Integration**\n\nI am now focusing on refining how the USC catalog is integrated. I\'m working on ensuring all `CATALOG_WORK_ITEMS` are correctly included. Previously, a small subset was hardcoded, which lead to issues. I am now making adjustments so that `createDefaultWorksForFloor` pulls directly from the full catalog for complete coverage and to retain user customizations.\n\n\n**Ensuring Full Data Integrity**\n\nI am now focusing on the implications for existing projects when updating to the full USC catalog. My concern is preventing data loss of user projects like \"Rumah 2 lantai\" during upgrades. I am evaluating the `migrateAndEnsureFullUscTemplate` function to ensure that all existing works, including custom additions and edits, are preserved and integrated correctly with the upgraded catalog.\n\n\n**Verifying Deletion Handling**\n\nI\'m now implementing the correct deletion flow. I\'m ensuring projects with the latest catalog version no longer auto-reinsert deleted items. For earlier versions, I am testing the migration logic and verifying that intentional item deletion is tracked and respected on subsequent project loads. This focuses on preventing accidental data restoration.\n\n\n\n**Refining Template Preservation**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the template and project preservation. I am implementing the outlined migration strategy, specifically handling catalog updates and user customizations. I am updating older projects and ensuring deleted items remain deleted, with added functionality to restore them. My aim is maintaining data integrity.\n\n\n**Validating Catalog Alignment**\n\nI\'m now methodically verifying the integrity of `CATALOG_WORK_ITEMS` in `/src/data/uscCatalog.ts`. I\'m meticulously comparing each item against the USC catalog and its corresponding JSON data to ensure every required element from the `RAB Gedung USC.xlsx` and the JSON is present and accounted for. My focus is on ensuring comprehensive coverage across all sections, including structural, architectural, and MEP elements.\n\n\n**Validating Structure Integration**\n\nI am now meticulously validating the architectural and MEP sections within the `/src/data/uscCatalog.ts` file, ensuring complete data integration. I am checking all elements from my notes, cross-referencing this against the work structure and confirming accurate data inclusion from RAB Gedung USC.xlsx, ensuring all structural elements are present and correctly accounted for, including those for the second floor, and other floors.\n\n\n"
