  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining Audit Findings**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the audit data. Specifically, I\'m analyzing the root causes behind the user\'s reported dimensions: 12.7m length, 6m width, 76.20 m\302\262 cleaning volume, and 37.4 bouwplank length. My aim is to understand the precise origins of these values and identify any discrepancies or areas for improvement.\n\n\n**Tracing Data Origins**\n\nI\'ve traced the project data\'s origin to legacy storage and AI image processing. The dimensions and volume are derived from a previous project stored in `ruangrab_projects_v1` within `localStorage`. This project was tied to testing a house plan with specific dimensions. The system transferred this legacy data, without user account association initially. This legacy data was copied to user accounts via a `loadAllProjects` function.\n\n\n**Uncovering Data Contamination**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed how legacy, unowned projects, specifically those from `ruangrab_projects_v1` containing the suspect dimensions, were copied into user accounts A and B. The `loadAllProjects` function transferred this contaminated data. The earlier `migrateProjectToFullUscTemplate` function, when checking `isOldUscDemoValue`, failed to strip these legacy projects. This led to persistent contaminated data in both local and server storage.\n\n\n**Addressing Server-Side Vulnerability**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the critical absence of robust server-side project persistence and ownership verification, specifically in the `/api/projects` area. The current reliance on `localStorage`, especially without proper `userId` separation and lack of backend validation, is a significant weakness. I\'m actively working to implement `/api/projects` endpoints with dedicated storage in `/data/projects_store.json` and `/data/ai_cache_store.json` to create more secure data handling on the server side. I\'m prioritizing the addition of endpoints with robust authentication requirements, too!\n\n\n**Securing User Data Access**\n\nI\'m now implementing strict backend checks for user data access. My primary focus is verifying `userId` through the `x-user-id` header or body. I\'ll rigorously ensure that a project\'s `ownerUid` matches the `userId` before allowing any operations. This will prevent any cross-account data leakage. Also, I\'m working to isolate and reset the AI Assistant Modal state and related data by incorporating `${userId}::${activeProjectId}` keys.\n\n\n**Clarifying Data Integrity**\n\nI\'m now integrating the user\'s specific guidelines regarding USC templates and user project data. I\'m focusing on implementing the distinctions outlined in Sections 1C and 1D. My goal is to guarantee that the `USC_STANDARD_TEMPLATE` remains read-only and free of dimension data. Concurrently, I\'m ensuring `USER_PROJECT_DATA` is correctly tied to `userId` and `projectId`, and that new projects adhere to the specifications. I\'m focusing on wiping any demo data which might pollute calculations.\n\n\n**Analyzing Data Cleaning**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on data scrubbing strategies within `projectStorage.ts` and the backend. The plan is to permanently eradicate the `LEGACY_STORAGE_KEY_PROJECTS` migration, thus preventing any future copies of `ruangrab_projects_v1` into user accounts, and I will also remove `localStorage.removeItem(\'ruangrab_projects_v1\')`. Also, I\'m developing strategies to detect and handle existing contaminated projects by checking for the \'isolationVersion\'. If it\'s not \'4.0.0-ISOLATED\', and the \'ownerUid\' doesn\'t align with the \'userId\', I\'ll exclude it as part of that particular user account\'s data.\n\n\n**Refining Data Scrubbing**\n\nI\'m now refining the data scrubbing logic during the upgrade to \'4.0.0-ISOLATED\'. I\'ll discard projects from other users or reset leaked demo values, specifically those originating from \'reference\' or showing the \'rencana 1-Model.pdf\' demo leak data. For detected demo values during upgrade, I\'ll reset them to null, preserving genuine user input.\n\n\n**Implementing Data Isolation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring data integrity within projects stamped with `\'4.0.0-ISOLATED\'`. Any further interaction will permanently associate all work with the current user\'s ID as the `ownerUid`. Additionally, I\'ve identified that `customCatalogItems` and `disabledCatalogIds` are globally stored in `localStorage`, so I\'m scoping them by the user ID to prevent cross-account contamination.\n\n\n**Structuring Category Systems**\n\nI\'m now implementing the category structure, confirming proper user interface rendering of \"Umum & Pondasi\", \"Lantai 1\", \"Lantai 2\" and \"Lantai berikutnya\" and accordion-style categories within each: \"Persiapan Dll\", \"Struktural\", \"Arsitektural\", and \"MEP\". The UI is correctly toggling category visibility when clicked. My focus is on data consistency.\n\n\n**Organizing Category Systems**\n\nI\'m now implementing the category system\'s behavior regarding interaction and states. Opening a category should not automatically open others or corrupt their states. I\'m focusing on displaying concise category headers: name, job count, filled/unfilled status, and an open/close arrow icon. I\'m also adding \"Show All\" and \"Minimalize\" buttons to control the view of the entire structure.\n\n\n**Designing Category System**\n\nI\'m now designing the category accordion drawers to integrate within the current floor structure in `WorkTable.tsx`. I\'ll render distinct category elements with interactive open/close toggles, correctly scoped per floor. I\'m focusing on ensuring category states are preserved and managed independently across different floors, ensuring no accidental state corruption.\n\n\n**Implementing Category Interactions**\n\nI\'m now integrating the category drawer behavior and global control logic. I will show categories according to works present in each floor, grouped in the canonical USC order, tracking the open/close state using the `${floor.id}::${category}` keys. I\'m focusing on providing users instant access to the category bars, with \"Show All\" and \"Minimalis\" buttons for each floor to control their state and to also include a floor selector at the top toolbar of the `WorkTable`.\n\n\n**Refining Category Display**\n\nI\'m now refining the category display logic, focusing on implementing the category drawer interaction. I\'ll render distinct accordion bars with open/close toggles, showing the count of \"Belum Diisi,\" \"Terisi/Terverifikasi,\" and \"Perlu Verifikasi (AI)\" jobs for each category and floor. I\'ll ensure state management per floor with \"Show All\" and \"Minimalis\" buttons at the top toolbar.\n\n\n\n**Analyzing AI-Assisted Section**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing the AI Assistant quantity takeoff optimization outlined in Section 3 of the prompt. Currently, I\'m working to classify PDF pages and images, supporting at least 16 image types. AI must separate reading contexts for multiple image parts within a single page, including the 16 types. I\'m prioritizing the classification and separation processes.\n\n\n**Implementing AI Reading Context**\n\nI\'m now implementing context-aware AI parsing of PDF and image data. I\'m focusing on ensuring data integrity by associating extracted values ( `AIExtractionItem` and `AIBeamSloofSegment`) with their originating page, drawing type, title, floor, grid location, element code, raw and converted dimensions/units, and source information. This ensures that the AI doesn\'t mix data from unrelated contexts.\n\n\n**Defining AI Quantity Takeoff**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining the AI\'s quantity takeoff process, particularly regarding beam and sloof calculations, based on scale and floor context. I\'ll prioritize accurately interpreting image-based dimensions, converting units, and avoiding incorrect scale application. My goal is to prevent the AI from incorrectly calculating total lengths, ensuring it accurately sums segment lengths for each element.\n\n\n**Refining Quantity Takeoff**\n\nI\'m now refining the AI\'s quantity takeoff, specifically for beams and sloofs, following the detailed steps. I\'m focusing on parsing grid/span segments to accurately calculate total lengths in meters. My aim is to prevent double-counting, ensuring the AI sums each segment correctly, by deduplicating segments with identical codes and locations. I\'m also ensuring segment details display in both the AI Assistant modal and work detail calculator, for user verification.\n\n\n**Integrating AI Results**\n\nI\'m now integrating AI-extracted data with the default USC jobs. I\'ll match AI findings with existing USC tasks and populate their parameters. I\'ll avoid job duplication, only adding new jobs for elements not in the default list. Also, I\'ll place these jobs into the correct floor and category, and ensure data integrity. Furthermore, I\'ll maintain transparency by exposing the image source for each AI-filled parameter.\n\n\n**Evaluating AI Readings**\n\nI\'m now evaluating the confidence levels of the AI\'s data extractions. I\'ll implement statuses like \'Terbaca Jelas\', \'Hasil Penggabungan\', \'Perlu Verifikasi\', and \'Tidak Ditemukan\', preventing auto-verification. Users will be able to edit, accept, or reject AI results. I\'m focusing on providing more control and transparency to the user.\n\n\n**Optimizing AI Data Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing AI data handling, especially regarding SHA-256 hashing and caching of `(userId, projectId)`-related document data. I\'m also planning to store results in `project.aiTakeoffHistory`. I\'ll create a clear progress display with the stages: document validation, image type classification, segment extraction, and mapping to the USC catalog. I\'ll support page range selection and/or staged analysis.\n\n\n"
